  {"type": "split", "photo": "sys", "h2": "Hybrid or all-electric? The Oshawa Power and Enbridge math",
   "html": "<p>Most Oshawa homes come to us with a working gas furnace and a dead or dying air conditioner, so the practical question is whether to keep the furnace. In almost every case the answer is yes, at least for now. A hybrid system lets the heat pump carry cooling and the heating from roughly October to April on most days, while the furnace fires only on the deep-cold nights when gas is the cheaper fuel.</p>"
           "<p>Where the furnace changes is the balance point, the outdoor temperature at which the thermostat hands over from heat pump to furnace. We set it from your actual Oshawa Power electricity rate and your Enbridge gas rate, not a rule of thumb. The colder it gets, the less heat the heat pump makes per dollar, and the balance point is where the two fuels cost the same.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Gas furnace in good shape: hybrid, with the furnace as backup</li><li>Furnace near the end of its life: hybrid with a new furnace, or an electric air handler if you want off gas</li><li>Baseboard-heated addition: ductless heat pump for that space, higher rebate tier may apply</li><li>Ducts too small to fix: ductless multi-zone instead of forcing a ducted unit</li></ul>"
           "<p>Changing the furnace at the same time raises the venting and chimney questions we cover on our <a href=\"/oshawa/\">Oshawa HVAC page</a>. For ratings, HSPF2 and backup options for homes anywhere in Ontario, read the province-wide <a href=\"/heat-pump/cold-climate/\">cold-climate heat pump</a> page.</p>"},
